\subsection{Recompute Offsets after Widening a Stencil}

When a branch displacement does not fit, replacing its fragment
with a larger form may invalidate another branch that previously
fit. Consider a toy library with a short and a universally reaching
long form for each relaxable branch. Both realize the same semantic
operation and location contract. Assume fixed fragment order, no
alignment padding, and enough private writable space for the final
image. The short displacement is relative to the end of its fragment.
Compute offsets from the current size choices, and allow widening
but never shortening. Do not write any relocations until the choices
stop changing.

\begin{sudocode}[Monotone stencil relaxation]
\State choose short form for each relaxable branch
\Repeat
  \State $O\gets$ layout of fragments with current sizes
  \State $W\gets\emptyset$
  \ForAll{branches $b$ still short}
    \State $d\gets O[target(b)]-(O[b]+size(b))$
    \If{$d$ is out of short range}
      \State add $b$ to $W$ \Comment{Use one layout per pass}
    \EndIf
  \EndFor
  \State widen all entries of $W$
\Until{$W$ is empty}
\State emit and patch using $O$ \Comment{Only the stable layout is valid}
\end{sudocode}

Give short branches size two and range $[-4,3]$, and long
branches size five. Lay out branch $A$, branch $B$, a one-byte
fragment, target $T$ of one byte, a five-byte fragment, then
target $U$. Let $A$ target $T$ and $B$ target $U$. Initially
$T=5$ and $U=11$; $A$ has displacement 3 and $B$ has 7.
Widen $B$. Now $T=8$ and the short displacement of $A$ is 6.
Widen $A$ as well. The stable targets are 11 and 17,
and the end-relative displacements of the two long branches
are 6 and 7. Decode the finished image and assert both destinations.

The fragment positions in \Cref{fig:visual-252} explain why widening \(B\) forces another pass: \(T\) moves three bytes while the end of short \(A\) has not moved yet.

