At publication, every displacement uses final offsets and fits its
selected form. With $B$ relaxable branches there are at most $B$
growing passes and one final check. A real library without a guaranteed
long form must report failure or select a different placement strategy.

\section{Where the fast path ends}

Dossier Chapter~14 sketches generating several engines from semantic opcode definitions; Chapter~24 discusses quickening the interpreter's bytecode view. Quickening changes which interpreter handler is selected; copy-and-patch selects and connects actual native fragments. A practical combination is to assign stable opcode IDs, generate both handlers and stencils from definitions, and keep independent conformance tests for traps and helper calls. This combines the dossier ideas without assuming that the stencil extractor itself verifies the opcode implementation.

Following the dossier's system blueprint, the execution plane seals code together with PC, stack and unwind metadata. Create it privately, finish all patches, perform the target's required instruction-cache synchronization and executable-memory transition, then publish the entry. If a helper throws or collects, its metadata must describe the native call site, not merely the start of the stencil containing it. Keep artifact identities sensitive to the library ABI, target features and generator revision (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-259}).

More stencil variants can save branches and spills but inflate the prebuilt library and generated instruction footprint. Fusing a common bytecode pair can eliminate intermediary loads; it cannot erase a possible exception between the pair or admit a branch into the middle without a compatible entry. A fair baseline experiment compares a one-stencil-per-op emitter against a small fused library and a direct single-pass emitter (Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-251}). Count library build time and resident bytes separately from runtime layout, relocation and publication latency. A fast \texttt{memcpy} alone is not a measurement of compilation.
